Track 2: Zohra Grafts the Solar Stem
- Story & Context: Squeezed between crates of grafted aloe and unsold patch cables inside the caravan's repair van at Wadi-Ramla, key-technician Zohra spent three days confessing her longing for the coastal cities she had abandoned. Her eyes burned from soldering iron resin and the bitter sap of euphorbia stems she used to seal leaking bellows gaskets on her accordion. Over a complex jazz-rock progression marked by weeping fretless bass and breezy whistle leads, she recorded her vulnerability straight to an eight-track cassette, sighing into a dust-choked microphone: "I traded your mother’s turquoise ring to hear the oscillators breathe." The track leaked across nomadic radio stations, becoming an unofficial anthem for runaway youths joining the synthesizer caravans.
